LSX-64 vs RV64I Performance Analysis Report
Cycle-Accate Simulator Benchmark Study
Date: August 12, 2026
Simulator: LSX-64 Cycle-Accurate Functional Simulator v1.0
Architectures Compared: LSX-64 (7-stage pipeline) vs RV64I (5-stage pipeline)
  

1. Executive Summary
This report presents a cycle-accurate performance comparison between the Loongson-inspired LSX-64 architecture and a standard RV64I (RISC-V 64-bit Integer) baseline. Eight benchmarks were executed on a Python-based cycle-accurate simulator that models the full fetch-decode-execute pipeline, register file forwarding, multi-cycle arithmetic, set-associative caches, and branch penalty mechanics of each architecture.
The LSX-64 design trades a deeper 7-stage pipeline (enabling higher clock frequencies in physical silicon) for increased branch penalties and CPI. On the simulator's equivalent clock model, LSX-64 achieves an average CPI of 1.727 versus RV64I's 1.512, yielding a mean speedup of 0.880x (i.e., LSX-64 requires ~12% more cycles at equal frequency). The performance gap is most pronounced in branch-heavy workloads (0.827x) and narrowest in compute-bound ALU chains (0.929x).
  

