5. Analysis
5.1 Pipeline Depth Impact
The 7-stage LSX-64 pipeline incurs a 3-cycle branch penalty versus RV64I's 2-cycle penalty. This 50% increase in penalty per taken branch is the dominant factor in the performance gap.
For the loop_sum benchmark (100 iterations, 99 taken branches):
* LSX-64 control stalls: 99 x 3 = 297 cycles
* RV64I control stalls: 99 x 2 = 198 cycles
* Difference: 99 cycles (explains most of the 608 vs 507 cycle gap)
The deeper pipeline's branch cost is particularly harmful because the benchmarks use predict-not-taken strategy, and loop branches are taken 93-99% of the time. Each taken branch flushes the entire front-end (3 stages for LSX-64 vs 2 for RV64I).
5.2 ALU-Bound Workloads
The alu_chain benchmark (no branches, no memory) shows the smallest gap (0.929x). The CPI difference (1.333 vs 1.238) is primarily from cache stalls (both architectures experience 2 L1-I misses at 100 cycles each). With no branches and no data hazards, both pipelines operate near their theoretical minimum CPI of 1.0, and the extra pipeline stages in LSX-64 contribute only marginally to overhead.
5.3 Multi-Cycle Operations
The mul_acc benchmark exercises the multi-cycle MUL unit (latency = 2 cycles). LSX-64 incurs 60 data stalls from MUL latency versus 0 for RV64I (which models MUL as single-cycle). This contributes an additional 0.250 CPI overhead. In physical silicon, the deeper pipeline allows LSX-64 to clock higher, partially compensating for the multi-cycle latency in wall-clock terms.
5.4 Memory System Performance
Cache stall contributions are similar between architectures since the cache hierarchy is identical. The memory_stride benchmark shows the highest cache stall count (924 total) due to stride-4 access patterns that reduce L1-D hit rates to 75%. The 32 data stalls in this benchmark come from load-use hazards, which affect both architectures equally (1-cycle stall each).
5.5 Branch-Heavy Workloads
The branch_heavy benchmark exhibits the largest performance gap (0.827x). With 125 branches (99 taken) and an if/else structure, the LSX-64 pipeline pays 297 cycles in control stalls versus 198 for RV64I. The 99-cycle difference accounts for 97% of the 101-cycle total gap (584 vs 483).
  

